# ==============================================================================
# Organize — Environment Configuration Template
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (or export the values in your shell profile /
# systemd EnvironmentFile) and adjust. A `.env` sitting next to the scripts is
# read automatically at startup by every tool; a value already exported in the
# environment always wins over the file.
#
# Nothing here is required to *run* the toolkit. The one optional networked
# feature (the OpenSubtitles fallback for image-only movies) is switched off
# until you set an API key, and everything else works offline. Every path
# default lives in each tool and is overridable per run with CLI flags (see the
# README).
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subtitle extraction (subtitle_extractor.py)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A text-based embedded English track (SRT/SSA/ASS/WebVTT/USF) is extracted to
# <movie>.eng.srt with MKVToolNix (mkvmerge + mkvextract on PATH). That path is
# entirely local. Image-based subtitles (PGS/VobSub/DVB) are never OCR'd: when
# a movie's English subtitles exist only as bitmaps, the movie is looked up on
# OpenSubtitles by its exact file hash and a matching English .srt is written
# beside it. Without a key, that step is skipped and the movie is reported for
# a human - nothing else changes.
#
# Create a free API key at https://www.opensubtitles.com/en/consumers (the key
# identifies the application, not the person).
# OPENSUBTITLES_API_KEY=
#
# Optional account. Signing in raises the daily download allowance from 5 per
# day per IP address to 20 (VIP accounts get more). Never commit real values.
# OPENSUBTITLES_USERNAME=
# OPENSUBTITLES_PASSWORD=
#
# Where the record of created sidecars lives. It must be OUTSIDE the media
# library; it is the provenance record of which sidecars this tool created, and
# whether each came from the movie's own track or an exact-hash download.
# Defaults to
# <folder holding the scripts>/ReportsAndLogs/subtitle_extractor_extracted.json.
# SUBTITLE_EXTRACTED_LEDGER=/mnt/storage/organize_logs/subtitle_extractor_extracted.json

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The movie library root (every tool)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORGANIZE_LIBRARY is the one setting that matters most: it is the canonical
# Jellyfin/Plex movie-library root, and EVERY tool in the toolkit honours it.
# Set it once here and a bare `python organize.py run` (or any
# individual tool) works on that library with no flags at all.
#
# Resolution order, highest first:
#   1. an explicit --source / --dir / --target flag
#   2. ORGANIZE_LIBRARY
#   3. MOVIE_STD_TARGET      (legacy name, still honoured)
#   4. the platform default  (Windows: E:\torrents\final_organized,
#                             Linux/macOS: ~/Media/Movies)
# ORGANIZE_LIBRARY=/mnt/storage/torrents/final_organized

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths (movie_standardizer.py)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The defaults assume the documented Windows layout (E:\torrents\...).
# On Windows, logs/reports/state cache default to
#   E:\torrents\tools\ReportsAndLogs\<tool>\...
# On Linux/macOS point these at your own folders. Requirements:
#   * SOURCE and TARGET must be on the SAME filesystem (hardlink-only ingest)
#   * logs/reports must live OUTSIDE the media library
# Batch-scan source root (no path argument given). Platform default:
#   Windows: E:\torrents\final    Linux/macOS: ~/torrents/final
# MOVIE_STD_SOURCE=/mnt/storage/torrents/final
# MOVIE_STD_TARGET=/mnt/storage/torrents/final_organized
# MOVIE_STD_LOG=/mnt/storage/organize_logs/movie_standardizer.log
# MOVIE_STD_REPORT=/mnt/storage/organize_logs/movie_standardizer_report.txt

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behavior (movie_standardizer.py)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum size in MB for a file to be treated as a feature movie (default 300).
# MOVIE_STD_MIN_SIZE=300

# Seconds to wait for the cross-tool coordination lock (default 60).
# MOVIE_STD_LOCK_TIMEOUT=60

# Duplicate-folder maintenance: REPORT (default, non-destructive),
# QUARANTINE, or DELETE.
# MOVIE_STD_MAINTENANCE_MODE=REPORT
# MOVIE_STD_QUARANTINE=/mnt/storage/quarantine

# Optional JSON run manifest (automation-friendly), outside source and target.
# MOVIE_STD_MANIFEST=/mnt/storage/organize_logs/manifest.json

# Enable the duplicate-folder scan (equivalent of --deduplicate).
# MOVIE_STD_DEDUPLICATE=false

# Preview-only placement (equivalent of --dry-run).
# MOVIE_STD_DRY_RUN=false

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Playback chain (audio_standardizer.py; the whole toolkit's defaults)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every default in this toolkit is tuned for ONE living-room chain:
#   Chromecast with Google TV (HD) G454V "boreal"
#     -> Samsung UN60F6350AF (2013, 1080p SDR)
#     -> Hisense AX3125H soundbar (via the TV's HDMI (ARC) port).
# See docs/hardware.md for the dossier. The wiring decides one thing: whether
# multichannel PCM (AAC 5.1, FLAC 7.1, ...) is acceptable as-is — the
# Chromecast decodes such tracks to PCM, and the soundbar's HDMI IN carries
# multichannel PCM — or whether such movies should be transcoded to AC-3
# because plain ARC / optical only carries stereo PCM.
#   tv-arc            (default; the as-shipped wiring: Chromecast -> TV HDMI,
#                      TV --ARC/optical--> soundbar)
#   soundbar-hdmi-in  upgrade wiring: Chromecast -> soundbar HDMI IN -> TV
# ORGANIZE_PLAYBACK_WIRING=tv-arc

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared state cache (library_auditor.py, mkv_track_cleaner.py, bitdepth.py,
# audio_standardizer.py, `organize.py status`)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A rebuildable SQLite cache of what each tool last decided, so `organize status`
# can answer "what is left to do?" without probing anything. It is never read as
# authority: every tool still re-derives its verdict from the live filesystem,
# and deleting the file costs one slow pass, never correctness.
#
# Where it lives (default: beside the logs and reports - never inside the media
# library). Each tool also accepts --state-db PATH.
# ORGANIZE_STATE_DB=/mnt/storage/organize_logs/state.db
#
# Turn it off everywhere at once (same as passing --no-state to each tool).
# ORGANIZE_NO_STATE=0
